      "knowledge_point": "Identifying True Statements Regarding Interiors and Boundaries of Sets",
      "kp_id": "e-11034"
    },
    "q-161651": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161651",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Consider the set $A,$ defined by \n\n$$\nA=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1<{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $A=∂A\\cup \\text{int}A$\n\nII. $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A$\n\nIII. $∂A⊂A$",
      "worked_solution": "The boundary of a set $A,$ denoted $∂A,$ is the set that contains all the boundary points of $A.$\n\nIn this case, the set $A$ and its boundary $∂A={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ are shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14245698/assets/a39ecae41a88e873fa9eecb30a5b554be9ec98cf630851715ab35f2f873710f2.png)\n\n   \n\nThe interior of a set $A,$ denoted $\\text{int}\\,A,$ is the set containing all the interior points of $A.$ It contains every point in $A$ except the boundary points: \n\n$$\n\\text{int}\\,A=A-∂A\n$$\n\nThe interior of $A$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14245698/assets/5e473584ef5193b434ff6c138b08c4fbd9111a37252019143b1afb2d48c913c6.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement:\n\n- Statements I and III are false. Since ${C}_{1}⊂∂A-A,$ we must have that $∂A⊄A$ and also that $A\\ne ∂A\\cup \\text{int}A.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{int}\\,A$ contains all points in $A$ except the boundary points, we must have $A-∂A=\\text{int}\\,A.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
